window.WIKI_INDEX = [{"title": "Idaho overview and state facts", "url": "/#overview", "text": "Idaho is a state in the northwestern United States. Its capital is Boise , and it joined the Union on July 3, 1890, as the 43rd state. Known as the Gem State , Idaho takes its nickname from the variety of gemstones found within its borders. Idaho borders six U.S. states and the Canadian province of British Columbia. Its landscapes include mountain ranges, river canyons, and the volcanic Snake River Plain. The U.S. Census Bureau estimated its population at 2,029,733 on July 1, 2025. Agriculture and ranching sit alongside manufacturing, food processing, technology, energy, and tourism in the state’s economy. State of Idaho The Gem State Flag of Idaho · Idaho.gov State facts Country United States Capital Boise Statehood July 3, 1890 43rd state Population 2,029,733 July 1, 2025 estimate Land area 82,645.14 sq mi 2020 Census geography Highest point Mount Borah 12,662 ft Governor Brad Little Verified Sept. 25, 2026 Motto Esto Perpetua “Be Eternal” Website idaho.gov"}, {"title": "Geography", "url": "/#geography", "text": "Idaho shares borders with Washington and Oregon to the west, Nevada and Utah to the south, and Montana and Wyoming to the east. British Columbia lies to the north. Portions of the western boundary follow the Snake River, while mountain divides shape much of the boundary with Montana. The Snake River Plain extends roughly 400 miles across the state. Its eastern portion preserves a record of volcanic activity associated with the Yellowstone hotspot’s track; Craters of the Moon contains younger lava fields within this landscape. Idaho.gov lists Mount Borah as the state’s highest point at 12,662 feet."}, {"title": "History", "url": "/#history", "text": "Indigenous peoples Idaho’s history begins long before territorial boundaries and statehood. Five federally recognized tribes retain ancestral homelands in the region: the Coeur d’Alene Tribe, Kootenai Tribe of Idaho, Nez Perce Tribe, Shoshone-Bannock Tribes, and Shoshone-Paiute Tribes. The Shoshone-Paiute homeland and reservation cross the present Idaho–Nevada border. These are living communities with distinct histories and governments. Territory and statehood Lewis and Clark crossed into the region at Lemhi Pass in 1805. Fur trading, missionary activity, and migration through the region followed. President Abraham Lincoln signed the act creating Idaho Territory on March 4, 1863. Idaho became a state on July 3, 1890. Selected milestones in Idaho history Year Event 1890 Idaho enters the Union as the 43rd state. 1910 The “Big Blowup” fires affect northern Idaho. 1936 The first ski chairlift opens in Idaho. 1951 Experimental Breeder Reactor I produces electricity from nuclear energy. 1976 The Teton Dam collapses. 2009 Idaho hosts the Special Olympics World Winter Games."}, {"title": "Population", "url": "/#population", "text": "The 2020 census counted 1,839,106 Idaho residents. The Census Bureau’s July 1, 2025 estimate was 2,029,733, a 10.4% increase from its adjusted April 1, 2020 estimates base. That base differs slightly from the original census count. Population at selected dates Reference date Population Measure April 1, 2010 1,567,582 Census count April 1, 2020 1,839,106 Census count July 1, 2025 2,029,733 Population estimate (Vintage 2025)"}, {"title": "Government", "url": "/#government", "text": "Idaho’s state government has three branches: executive, legislative, and judicial. The governor leads the executive branch; the legislature includes a Senate and a House of Representatives; and the judiciary administers the state court system. As verified on September 25, 2026, the governor is Brad Little . USAGov’s Idaho directory connects residents to state agencies, elected officials, local governments, and services such as education, elections, motor vehicles, and health and welfare. Official information is available through Idaho.gov ."}, {"title": "Economy and natural resources", "url": "/#economy", "text": "Idaho Commerce identifies food processing, manufacturing, semiconductors, technology, aerospace, energy, outdoor recreation, and tourism among the state’s key industry sectors. This mix connects agricultural production with industrial and technical activity. Agriculture, ranching, forests, and water are central to Idaho’s natural-resource economy. State programs address food safety, soil and water conservation, forest management, environmental quality, and energy and mineral resources. Idaho.gov’s natural-resources directory brings these agencies and programs together."}, {"title": "Top Businesses", "url": "/#top-businesses", "text": "Selected Idaho businesses and institutions span technology, health care, grocery retail, higher education, agriculture, and energy. Featured businesses and institutions Organization Overview and Idaho connection Micron Technology Semiconductors and technology. Founded in Boise in 1978, Micron develops memory and storage products used in computers, mobile devices, and data centers. St. Luke’s Health System Health care. An Idaho-based nonprofit health system that provides care through a connected network of hospitals, clinics, and health professionals. Albertsons Companies Grocery retail. A grocery business with roots in Boise, where the first Albertsons store opened at 16th and State streets. Boise State University Higher education and research. Located in Idaho’s capital, Boise State provides undergraduate and graduate education, research, and community programs. J.R. Simplot Company Food and agriculture. The family-owned Simplot business is headquartered in Boise, with operations including food processing, farming, ranching, and fertilizer manufacturing. Idaho Power (IDACORP) Electric utility. Headquartered in Boise, Idaho Power supplies electricity in Idaho and Oregon. It is the primary subsidiary of IDACORP , its publicly traded parent company."}, {"title": "Education", "url": "/#education", "text": "Idaho’s education system includes public schools, colleges and universities, community colleges, and career and technical programs. The Department of Education supports K–12 education, while the State Board of Education coordinates statewide education policy, including higher education. State resources include school data, college directories, scholarships, financial aid, and career planning. The Idaho.gov education directory provides a starting point for students and families."}, {"title": "Parks and recreation", "url": "/#recreation", "text": "Idaho’s outdoor destinations include Hells Canyon, Shoshone Falls, Sun Valley, and Craters of the Moon. River recreation, skiing, wildlife observation, and camping are among the activities highlighted by the state’s official website. Craters of the Moon offers an example of the state’s volcanic landscape, with lava flows and volcanic features on the Snake River Plain. The Idaho Department of Parks and Recreation and Visit Idaho provide park and visitor information."}, {"title": "State symbols", "url": "/#symbols", "text": "Idaho’s official symbols reflect its plants, wildlife, and mineral resources. Its flag, adopted in 1907, places the state seal on a blue field above a banner bearing the state’s name. Selected official symbols Symbol Designation Bird Mountain bluebird Flower Syringa Tree Western white pine Fruit Wild huckleberry Fish Cutthroat trout Gem Star garnet"}, {"title": "International connections", "url": "/#international", "text": "The Department of State’s 2017–2021 archived Idaho profile describes international exchange programs, university partnerships, salmon-resource cooperation with Canada, and export activity. It reports approximately $4 billion in goods exports in 2018 and 86,038 passports issued in fiscal year 2018 . These are historical figures from the archive, not present-day totals."}, {"title": "References and citations", "url": "/#references", "text": "Sources checked September 25, 2026. Statistical reference years are stated beside the figures. ↑ Idaho.gov. Facts & Symbols . Capital, statehood date, motto, elevation, state symbols, and flag. ↑ State of Idaho. Official state website . State nickname, elected officials, and recreation highlights. ↑ U.S. Census Bureau. QuickFacts: Idaho . July 1, 2025 population estimate; 2010 and 2020 censuses; 2020 land area. ↑ Idaho Transportation Department. Historic Survey of Roads in Idaho, Volume 1 . Geographic boundaries; see Section 2, Geography. PDF. ↑ National Park Service. Geodiversity Atlas: Craters of the Moon . Snake River Plain and volcanic landscapes. ↑ Idaho Department of Education. Indian Education . The five federally recognized tribes with ancestral homelands in Idaho. ↑ Library of Congress. Today in History: March 4 — Idaho . Lewis and Clark, migration, and the creation of Idaho Territory. ↑ Idaho.gov. History . Statehood and selected historical milestones. ↑ Idaho.gov. Government Organization & Structure . Executive, legislative, and judicial branches. ↑ USAGov. Idaho . Governor and directory of government agencies and services. ↑ Idaho Commerce. Key Industries . Manufacturing, food processing, technology, energy, and other industry sectors. ↑ Idaho.gov. Natural Resources . Agriculture, ranching, forests, water, and environmental agencies. ↑ Idaho.gov. Education . Schools, higher education, technical education, and student resources. ↑ U.S. Department of State. Idaho — 2017–2021 archive . Historical international partnerships, 2018 exports, and FY2018 passport figures. Archived; not current statistics. ↑ Micron Technology. About Micron . Memory and storage technology and the company’s origins in Boise. ↑ St. Luke’s Health System. About St. Luke’s . Idaho-based nonprofit health system and its hospitals and clinics. ↑ Albertsons. About Us . Grocery retail and the first Albertsons store in Boise. ↑ Boise State University. About Boise State . University location, academic programs, research, and community engagement. ↑ Simplot Company. About the Simplot Company . Boise headquarters and food and agriculture operations. ↑ IDACORP. At a Glance . Idaho Power’s relationship to IDACORP and its Idaho and Oregon service area."}, {"title": "Resources and external links", "url": "/resources/", "text": "Official sources for learning about Idaho, finding public services, and checking the facts in this article. Primary resources Idaho.gov The official state portal, with facts, history, government information, and resident services. USAGov: Idaho A federal directory of Idaho’s governor, agencies, elected officials, and public services. U.S. Department of State: Idaho 2017–2021 archive Historical information about international partnerships, exchanges, exports, and passports. Figures retain their original dates; the archive is no longer updated. Shared Google link: Idaho Resolves to a Google search for Idaho. Included as a discovery link; factual citations point to the underlying official sources. Research and statistics U.S. Census Bureau: Idaho QuickFacts Population, land area, and demographic statistics. The article uses the July 1, 2025 population estimate and labels the 2020 land-area measure. Idaho State Historical Society State history, archives, museums, and historical research. Library of Congress: Idaho Territory Historical context for the creation of the territory on March 4, 1863. Idaho Department of Education: Indian Education Information about Idaho’s tribal communities and education resources. Idaho Commerce: Key Industries Official descriptions of the state’s principal industry sectors. Education, nature, and visitor information Education in Idaho Schools, college directories, student support, and career planning. Idaho’s natural resources Agriculture, forests, water, wildlife, and environmental agencies. Idaho Department of Parks and Recreation State parks and recreation information. Visit Idaho Idaho’s official tourism resource. Source notes This independent reference is not an official state publication. The article’s numbered references identify the sources supporting its claims. The state flag image is reproduced without alteration from Idaho.gov’s Facts & Symbols page . Government officeholders were checked on September 25, 2026. Population estimates, census counts, and archived economic figures refer to different dates and should be read with their stated years. The land-area entry uses the Census Bureau’s 2020 measurement to avoid mixing it with other geographic measures."}, {"title": "Revision history", "url": "/history/", "text": "This page records revisions to the Idaho Wiki article. For the history of the state itself, see Idaho: History . Two revisions recorded. Current edition updated September 25, 2026. September 25, 2026 Top Businesses added Added a Top Businesses section after Economy and natural resources, featuring Micron Technology, St. Luke’s Health System, Albertsons Companies, Boise State University, J.R. Simplot Company, and Idaho Power (IDACORP). Included official links, descriptions, and six source references; updated the contents and search index. Read Top Businesses September 25, 2026 Initial edition Adapted the supplied biography wiki template into an Idaho state reference. Added the state infobox, geographic and historical overview, population data, government, economy, education, recreation, symbols, and source citations. Included a resources directory and dated historical figures from the Department of State archive. Verified population and land-area measures against the U.S. Census Bureau. About this edition This is a static reference website. The history records changes to the article; there are no public editing accounts or archived snapshots to compare."}];
